.nr HY 0
.ds AD l
.TH ORGWARRIOR 1 "" "orgwarrior 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
orgwarrior \- your Org agenda from the command line, run by your Emacs daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B orgwarrior
[\fIVIEW\fR]
.br
.B orgwarrior
\fIID\fR... \fIVERB\fR [\fIARGS\fR]
.br
.B orgwarrior add
[\fIKEYWORD\fR] \fItitle\fR [\fI:tag:\fR] [\fIs:DATE\fR] [\fId:DATE\fR]
.br
.B orgwarrior help
[\fITOPIC\fR | \fB\-\-man\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
A taskwarrior\-style command line for Org mode's agenda. Every task gets a short ID, and orgwarrior ID VERB acts on it. orgwarrior doesn't read your org files itself. It sends each command to your running Emacs daemon with emacsclient, and Org does the work (org\-agenda, org\-todo, org\-schedule, org\-read\-date). Your agenda files, TODO keywords, custom agenda commands, logging and habits all work as they do in Emacs. orgwarrior loads its own code into the daemon, so you don't need to change your init file.
.PP
IDs are short hashes of a task's file and title, so they're the same in every view and from one day to the next. Changing a keyword, tags or dates keeps the ID. Renaming or refiling a task changes it, and the new one is printed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PD 0
.SS Views
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior
the default view (today)
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior week
this week; week +1w, week 2026\-11\-02
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior fri
one day; +2d, 2026\-10\-15
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior all
every open TODO
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior wait
tasks in a state; next +@phone with a tag
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior +@phone
tasks with a tag; +a\-b, '+a|b'
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior flu shot
search the text ('"fri"' for a date word)
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior inbox
a view from [views] in your config
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior projects.org
a file's outline
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior clocked week
time clocked
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior closed week
what you finished, archived tasks included
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior \-\-archive flu shot
search the archives too
.SS Tasks
.PP
c1 is the ID beside each task.
.sp
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1
show the task
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 done
set any TODO keyword; nokeyword removes it
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 sched fri
schedule it; due DATE sets a deadline
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 when fri 14:00
move an event
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 +@home \-someday
add and remove tags
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 pri A
set the priority; up, down, none
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 note TEXT
add a note; done \-m NOTE with a state
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 refile projects
refile under a heading; alone, lists them
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 up | down
move among its siblings; promote, demote
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 rename NEW TITLE
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 effort 0:30
set the effort; prop NAME VALUE for others
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 clock in
start the clock; clock out stops it
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 clocks
list its clocks; clocks add logs past time
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 archive | kill
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 edit | open
open it in Emacs in this terminal, or a link
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior c1 c2 done
act on several tasks at once
.SS Adding
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior add NEXT call mom :@phone: s:fri d:+2w
.TP 32
.B orgwarrior add event Dentist @fri 14:00\-15:00
.PD
.SH COMMANDS
.SS views
.nf
.B orgwarrior [VIEW]
.fi
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior             the default view (default_view in config, or today)
orgwarrior today       today's agenda
orgwarrior week        this week's agenda
orgwarrior week DATE   the week of DATE: week +1w, week 2026\-11\-02
orgwarrior all         every open TODO
orgwarrior DATE        one day's agenda: fri, +2d, 2026\-10\-15 (help dates)
orgwarrior +@phone     a tag or property search, in org's syntax
orgwarrior +a\-b        tagged a and not b
orgwarrior '+a|b'      tagged a or b (quote the |)
orgwarrior KEYWORD...  tasks in those TODO states (wait; done; next active)
orgwarrior next +@phone
                       a state and a tag together
orgwarrior WORDS       anything else searches headings and text, ignoring
                       case and matching parts of words (org's C\-c a s)
orgwarrior '"fri"'     in quotes, always a search, for words that are
                       also dates
orgwarrior todo \-SCHEDULED={.+}\-DEADLINE={.+}
                       TODOs with no date (properties match too)
orgwarrior NAME        a view from [views] in your config (NAME = "KEY"
                       runs that org\-agenda custom command)
orgwarrior projects.org
                       a file's outline: its top\-level headings in file
                       order, done ones included.  For a file in a folder
                       of org\-directory, use work/notes.org, or just
                       notes.org if there's only one.
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each task has a short ID on the left, and task commands take it.  IDs stay the same in every view and from day to day.  Renaming a task gives it a new one.
.PP
\-\-archive includes the archive files, like v A in org's agenda:
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior \-\-archive flu shot      search everything ever archived
orgwarrior \-\-archive '+@phone'     a tag match; clocked \-\-archive week too
.fi
.RE
.PP
You can show an archived task by its ID, but you can't change it.
.PP
A habit (STYLE: habit, from org\-habit) shows org's consistency graph on the line below it.  Each cell is a day: * when it was done, ! for today, colored the way your Emacs theme colors it (help colors).
.SS show
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID...
.fi
.PP
Shows the task the way org opens it.  First where it is (file \[u203A] outline path), then the heading, the planning line, and the text, with each drawer (LOGBOOK, PROPERTIES) folded to one line.  Then its notes, newest first (help notes), and its child headings with their IDs, so you can show or change those next.  Links look the way Emacs shows them, and orgwarrior ID open lists and opens them (help open).  Several IDs show one after another.
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior ID \-\-drawers   unfold the drawers and show everything as it is
                          in the file
.fi
.RE
.PP
A task in a file that isn't writable (chmod a\-w) is read only, and its location line says so.  This is meant for a calendar that a script exports to org, where the next export would overwrite your changes anyway.  It's shown everywhere, but every change is refused, and it's never offered as a place to refile or add to.
.SS keywords
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID... KEYWORD [\-m NOTE]
.fi
.PP
Sets a TODO keyword, any of yours, in any case: orgwarrior c1 done, orgwarrior c1 7f next.  If the task's file has its own #+TODO line, those keywords apply.  Any heading can take one, including a plain heading or an event.  nokeyword removes it (like C\-c C\-t SPC), leaving a plain heading.
.PP
Logging follows your Emacs settings.  Where org would ask for a note, orgwarrior asks too (help notes).  Repeating tasks repeat as they do in Emacs.
.SS dates
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID... sched DATE
.B orgwarrior ID... due DATE
.fi
.PP
DATE is anything org\-read\-date understands:
.RS 2
.nf
fri, mon          the next Friday, Monday
+2d, +1w, +3m     counted from today
today, tomorrow
2026\-10\-15
15                the next 15th
fri 14:00         with a time
fri 14:00\-15:30   a time range (or fri 14:00+1:30)
none              remove the date
.fi
.RE
.PP
After the date, you can add a repeater and a warning period, as org writes them:
.RS 2
.nf
fri .+1w          repeat (+1w, .+1w, ++1w)
2026\-11\-01 \-3d    warn 3 days before
.fi
.RE
.PP
On its own, +1w is a date (a week from today).  A new date keeps the repeater that's already there, as in Emacs.  A whole timestamp is used as written, so sched "<2026\-10\-16 14:00>" drops the repeater.
.PP
orgwarrior ID... when DATE
.PP
Sets an event's date.  That's the plain timestamp on its own line under the heading (after any planning line and drawers), which is where add event puts it.  when changes it, or adds one if there isn't one, and none removes it.  A timestamp further down belongs to the text and is left alone.  DATE works the same as for sched, repeaters included.  A warning period does nothing on a plain timestamp.
.PP
The same dates work for views (orgwarrior fri) and for add (s:fri d:+2w).
.SS tags
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID... +TAG \-TAG
.B orgwarrior ID... tag +TAG \-TAG
.fi
.PP
Adds and removes tags: orgwarrior c1 +@phone \-someday.  Tags in a #+tags: { } group exclude each other, like with C\-c C\-q, so +@home removes @work.
.PP
After an ID, +/\- change tags.  At the start of the line, they search (orgwarrior +@phone, see help views).  Tags from the file's #+filetags belong to the file, so refile the task to change those.
.SS priority
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID... pri A
.B orgwarrior ID... pri up
.B orgwarrior ID... pri none
.fi
.PP
Sets the priority cookie ([#A]) with org\-priority, so it uses your range, from org\-priority\-highest to org\-priority\-lowest (A B C by default, or numbers).  up and down step it like S\-up and S\-down in org, which removes it past either end.  none removes it.
.SS rename
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID rename NEW TITLE
.fi
.PP
Changes the title.  The keyword, priority, tags and [1/3] cookies stay. Everything after rename is the title, and you don't need quotes (except around characters your shell treats specially).  The ID changes with the title, so the new one is printed:
.RS 2
.nf
6a \[u2192] 91  TODO call mom about the trip
.fi
.RE
.PP
A title can't start with a keyword or end in :tags:, since org would read them as a keyword and tags.  Use those commands instead.
.SS refile
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID... refile [TARGET]
.fi
.PP
Moves the tasks under a heading from your org\-refile\-targets, like C\-c C\-w, or to the top level of a file (TARGET projects.org, for files with targets up to level 1).  TARGET can be the heading's name, or any unique part of it or of its "file \[u203A] path".  A heading wins over a file with the same name. With no TARGET, it lists the targets.
.PP
The ID changes with the file, so the new one is printed:
.RS 2
.nf
6a \[u2192] 91  TODO call mom  \[u2192] tasks.org \[u203A] Tasks
.fi
.RE
.PP
A task that's already there stays put, and the others move.  Set keywords, dates and tags first, while the ID on your screen still works.
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior ID... refile \-\-new TITLE [WHERE]
.fi
.RE
.PP
first makes a plain heading called TITLE (a new project, say) as the last child of WHERE, then refiles into it.  WHERE is a file from your org\-refile\-targets (the heading goes at its top level) or one of the targets.  Without WHERE, it uses [refile] new_in from the config, and without that it lists the places.  [refile] new_suffix is added after the title (" [/]" gives a count of its tasks).
.PP
A shell function makes a shortcut that keeps the IDs first:
.RS 2
.nf
owr() { orgwarrior "$@" refile tasks; }
.fi
.RE
.SS up
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID up
.B orgwarrior ID down
.fi
.PP
Moves the task, with its subtree, one place up or down among its siblings (the headings at its level under the same parent), like M\-up and M\-down in Emacs.  It stays under the same parent.  To move it somewhere else, refile it.
.PP
orgwarrior ID demote orgwarrior ID promote
.PP
demote puts the task and its subtree under the heading just above it, as that heading's last child (M\-S\-right).  promote takes it out of its parent and puts it right after the parent.  Org's M\-S\-left promotes in place, which would turn the headings after it into its children.  promote moves it below them first, so only the task and its subtree move.
.SS archive
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID... archive
.B orgwarrior archive ID...
.fi
.PP
Archives the tasks, each with its subtree, like C\-c C\-x C\-s.  They go to org\-archive\-location (or an ARCHIVE property), FILE.org_archive by default. Archived tasks leave the agenda, so their IDs stop working.  orgwarrior \-\-archive WORDS finds them again, and you can show one by the ID it prints, but not change it.  To bring one back, open the archive file in Emacs.
.PP
A shortcut: alias owar='orgwarrior archive'
.SS kill
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID... kill [\-\-yes]
.B orgwarrior kill ID... [\-\-yes]
.fi
.PP
Deletes the tasks, each with its subtree, like C\-k in the agenda.  There's no undo, so it shows what will be deleted and asks first.  \-\-yes skips the question, and you need it when you're not at a terminal.
.PP
For tasks you're not going to do, use a done keyword (like SKIP, if you have one) instead.  kill is for junk.
.SS edit
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID edit
.B orgwarrior edit ID
.fi
.PP
Opens the task in Emacs, in this terminal: a frame of the daemon (emacsclient \-t), narrowed to the task and its subtree.  Edit the text, tick checkboxes (C\-c C\-c), anything you like.
.RS 2
.nf
C\-x C\-s    save
C\-x C\-c    close (asks to save first)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Changes you don't save are thrown away.
.SS open
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID open [N]
.B orgwarrior open ID [N]
.fi
.PP
Opens a link from the task (its heading or text) in your browser.  With only one link, it opens right away.  Otherwise pick from the list, or give the link's number:
.RS 2
.nf
[1] the order  https://shop.example/123
[2] https://tracking.example/abc
.fi
.RE
.PP
Over SSH, or without a graphical session, it prints the list instead of opening anything.  Most terminals make the URLs clickable.  file: links and other links only Emacs can open are listed, and orgwarrior ID edit opens them in Emacs.
.PP
A shortcut: alias owo='orgwarrior open'
.SS clock
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID clock in [TIME]
.B orgwarrior [ID] clock out [TIME]
.B orgwarrior clock [\-\-short]
.fi
.PP
clock in starts the clock on a task, like I in org's agenda.  Org writes the CLOCK line (where org\-clock\-into\-drawer says) and stops any clock that's running.  clock on its own shows what's running and since when.  \-\-short prints one line for a status bar, and nothing when no clock is running (in tmux: #(orgwarrior clock \-\-short)).
.PP
TIME is for when you forgot to start or stop the clock.  It can be a time of day, how long ago, or a date and a time.  Without it, the time is now. You can clock out later than now (+10m), but not clock in, since the clock would be running before it started.
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior k3 clock in 9:40          orgwarrior clock out 17:30
orgwarrior k3 clock in \-15m          orgwarrior clock out +10m
orgwarrior clock out yesterday 5:30pm
.fi
.RE
.PP
The running clock is the open CLOCK line in your files, not something Emacs keeps in memory.  Restarting Emacs loses nothing, and a clock started on another machine shows up once your files sync.  A clock running longer than 4 hours is flagged "still on it?".  If two are running (from two machines), clock lists both, and ID clock out stops one. orgwarrior [ID] clock cancel removes a clock you started by mistake.
.PP
orgwarrior ID clocks orgwarrior ID clocks N START\-END | N START\- | N \-END | N rm orgwarrior ID clocks add START\-END | START DURATION
.PP
clocks lists the task's clocks, newest first and numbered.  With a number, it changes that clock's start, end or both, or rm deletes it.  A time of day is on that clock's own date, and an end before the start (overnight) is on the next day.  A date given first applies to both.  Org recalculates the => total, as after S\-up on a CLOCK line in Emacs.
.PP
clocks add logs time you already spent, without touching a running clock. (clock in at a past time would stop the running clock at that time.)
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior k3 clocks 2 9:30\-12:20
orgwarrior k3 clocks 1 \-17:45
orgwarrior k3 clocks 2 2026\-10\-19 9:30\-12:20
orgwarrior k3 clocks add yesterday 14:00\-15:30
orgwarrior k3 clocks add 9:00 1h30m
.fi
.RE
.SS clocked
.nf
.B orgwarrior clocked [SPAN] [MATCH]
.fi
.PP
Shows the time clocked in SPAN across your agenda files, counted the way org's clocktable (C\-c C\-x C\-r) counts it.  It lists each file, its headings by depth, and the time in each, against the Effort where there is one.
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior clocked                 today
orgwarrior clocked week            this week; lastweek too
orgwarrior clocked month           this month; lastmonth, year
orgwarrior clocked 2026\-10\-20      a day; 2026\-W43 a week, 2026\-10 a month
orgwarrior clocked 2026\-10\-01..2026\-10\-15
orgwarrior clocked week +@phone    only tasks matching a tag
.fi
.RE
.PP
A [views] entry or a tab on the web page can be a report: clocked week. \-\-archive counts the archive files too.
.SS closed
.nf
.B orgwarrior closed [SPAN]
.fi
.PP
Shows what you finished in SPAN: each task with the time it was closed (its CLOSED stamp, set by a done state), day by day, the way org's agenda shows it in log mode (C\-u C\-u l).  The archive files are always included, since most finished tasks end up there.  SPAN works the same as for clocked:
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior closed                  today
orgwarrior closed week             this week; lastweek too
orgwarrior closed month            this month; lastmonth, year
orgwarrior closed 2026\-10\-20       a day; 2026\-W43 a week, 2026\-10 a month
orgwarrior closed 2026\-10\-01..2026\-10\-15
.fi
.RE
.PP
A repeating task marked done doesn't get a CLOSED stamp (org reschedules it instead), so it won't show up here.  A [views] entry or a tab on the web page can be one: closed week.
.SS properties
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID... prop NAME VALUE
.B orgwarrior ID... prop NAME none
.B orgwarrior ID... effort DURATION | none
.fi
.PP
Sets a property in the task's drawer, like C\-c C\-x p, or removes it with none.  The value is everything after the name:
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior k3 prop LOCATION the bank
orgwarrior k3 prop STYLE habit
.fi
.RE
.PP
Org's own properties (TODO, SCHEDULED, TAGS...) have their own commands.
.PP
Effort is your time estimate, like C\-c C\-x e sets it.  It takes a duration org can read (0:30, 1:15, 2h), and one of the Effort_ALL values where those are set.  Clocked time is shown against it.  orgwarrior ID shows the drawer.
.SS add
.nf
.B orgwarrior add [KEYWORD] title :tag: s:DATE d:DATE
.fi
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior add call the dentist
orgwarrior add NEXT call dentist :@phone: s:fri d:+2w
.fi
.RE
.PP
KEYWORD defaults to the first open keyword of the file it's going to (TODO, out of the box).  :a:b: adds tags, and s: and d: set SCHEDULED and DEADLINE (help dates).  They can go anywhere on the line.  A time, range, repeater or warning right after a date goes with it: s:fri 14:00\-15:00 +1w.
.PP
Lines after the first become the text:
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior add "$(printf 'pack :travel:\en\- [ ] socks\en\- [ ] charger')"
.fi
.RE
.PP
A heading in the text (* book flights) becomes a child heading, keeping its level.  Put quotes around a title with shell characters in it: orgwarrior add "why?"
.PP
Where it goes is set by [add] in the config (help config).  By default it's the end of org\-default\-notes\-file.  \-\-to PLACE puts it somewhere else: under a heading from your refile targets, or at a file's top level, named the way refile names them.  \-\-under ID puts it under any heading, by its ID:
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior add buy paint \-\-to reno
orgwarrior add order tiles \-\-under 4d
.fi
.RE
.PP
orgwarrior add event title @DATE [:tags:]
.RS 2
.nf
orgwarrior add event Dentist @fri 14:00\-15:00
orgwarrior add event Mom's birthday @2026\-11\-03 +1y
.fi
.RE
.PP
An event has no keyword, and its date is a plain timestamp.  @DATE takes anything sched does, including ranges and repeaters.  Events go where [add.event] in the config says, or where tasks go.  orgwarrior ID when DATE changes the date later (help dates).
.SS notes
.nf
.B orgwarrior ID... note TEXT
.B orgwarrior ID... done [\-m NOTE]
.fi
.PP
note adds a note any time, like org's "Note taken on", wherever org\-log\-into\-drawer puts it.  Everything after note is the text, and you don't need quotes.  Without text, it asks for it in the terminal.  Several IDs all get the same note.
.PP
Where Emacs would ask for a note (@ in org\-todo\-keywords, org\-log\-done set to note, or a LOGGING property), orgwarrior asks in the terminal:
.RS 2
.nf
Note (blank for none):
.fi
.RE
.PP
Leave it blank to log just the time.  Ctrl\-C cancels and changes nothing. \-m NOTE gives the note up front.  When it's not running in a terminal, it doesn't ask and adds no note.  The note goes through org, so org\-after\-note\-stored\-hook runs as it does in Emacs.
.SS config
.nf
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/orgwarrior/config.toml (usually ~/.config/orgwarrior/, or
.B wherever ORGWARRIOR_CONFIG points).  Everything is optional:
.fi
.RS 2
.nf
default_view = "today"   the view plain orgwarrior shows
socket = "server"        the emacsclient socket
prefix_format = "..."    an org\-agenda\-prefix\-format just for orgwarrior
tags = "inline"          or "right", as in Emacs
time_grid = false        show org's agenda time grid
color = "auto"           or "always", "never"
.fi
.RE
.RS 2
.nf
[views]                  name = "agenda key"
dash = "d"
.fi
.RE
.RS 2
.nf
[add]                    where orgwarrior add writes
file = "~/org/inbox.org" (default: org\-default\-notes\-file)
heading = "Tasks"        file under this heading (default: top level)
captured = true          stamp a :CAPTURED: time
.fi
.RE
.RS 2
.nf
[add.event]              where add event writes, with the same keys
file = "~/org/calendar.org"
heading = "Events"       (default: [add]'s file)
.fi
.RE
.RS 2
.nf
[refile]                 for refile \-\-new, and a front end's first choice
new_in = "projects.org"  where refile \-\-new makes its heading
new_suffix = " [/]"      added after its title
usual = "tasks"          the target offered first
.fi
.RE
.RS 2
.nf
[colors]                 see orgwarrior help colors
.fi
.RE
.RS 2
.nf
[web]                    for orgwarrior\-web (orgwarrior ignores it)
name = "Sam"             whose list it is; [web.tabs] sets the tabs
                         (label = query), [web.calendar] the calendar
                         feed
.fi
.RE
.SS colors
.nf
.B [colors] in the config maps a role or a TODO keyword to any of: bold, dim,
.B italic, underline; black, red, green, yellow, blue, magenta, cyan, white;
.B bright\-red and the like; #rrggbb (24\-bit); on\-green or on\-#rrggbb for a
.B background; or "none".
.fi
.RS 2
.nf
NEXT = "bold cyan"
id = "blue"
todo = "bold #ff8f88"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Plain colors follow your terminal's theme.  Color is off when the output is piped, when NO_COLOR is set, or with color = "never".
.PP
Roles:
.RS 2
.nf
id header date today time extra grid now overdue deadline upcoming done\-item
priority tag todo done heading label drawer timestamp link checkbox
habit\-clear habit\-clear\-future habit\-ready habit\-ready\-future habit\-alert
habit\-alert\-future habit\-overdue habit\-overdue\-future
.fi
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-m " NOTE"
A note to add with done, wait or any other keyword (help notes).
.TP
.BI \-s " SOCKET"
The Emacs server socket's name, if it isn't the default. You can also set socket in the config, or ORGWARRIOR_SOCKET.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show the help. After a command, show that command's help: orgwarrior add \-\-help.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Check your setup: Emacs and its server, your init file, your agenda files, where add writes and your config, saying what to fix. Exits 1 if anything has to be fixed.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/orgwarrior/config.toml
The config file, which is optional (default ~/.config/orgwarrior/config.toml). See the config topic above.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ORGWARRIOR_CONFIG
Use this config file instead of the default.
.TP
.B ORGWARRIOR_SOCKET
The Emacs server socket's name.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Turn off color, like color = "never".
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success. 1 when Emacs or Org refuses (an unknown ID, a bad date) or no daemon answers. 2 when orgwarrior can't understand the command line.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR emacs (1),
.BR emacsclient (1);
orgwarrior-web, the same agenda as a web page: https://github.com/aleksozolins/orgwarrior\-web
.SH BUGS
Reported at https://github.com/aleksozolins/orgwarrior/issues.
